    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §33-1001, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4363A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to requiring\nlawn care companies to provide information to the property owner prior to\nany commercial lawn application\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nThe purpose of this bill is to amend\nprovisions regarding notice requirements from certain pesticide\napplicators.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nThis bill would permit owners or their\nagents to decide if information pertaining to the pesticides\nscheduled to be applied to their lawns should be in written, digital\nor electronic format, provided that commercial lawn pesticide\napplicators must have a written copy in their possession.\n\nEFFECTS OF PRESENT LAW WHICH THIS BILL WOULD ALTER:\nAmends §33-1001 of the Environmental Conservation Law\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nCurrently, the statutory language pertaining to\ncommercial lawn applicators limits them to providing only a written\ncopy in at least 12 point type to property owners or their\nagents. This is contrary to other sections of the Environmental\nConservation Law, in which the statutory language permits information\nto be supplied either in written, digital or electronic format.\n\nThis bill would allow, at the customer's option, for the conservation\nof resources by allowing the applicator to provide pesticide\ninformation electronically.\n\nCurrent law already allows such electronic transmission for pesticide\napplications indoors. This bill would extend this method to\ncommercial law applications.\n\nAllowing customers, or their agents, to choose to receive this\ninformation in an environmentally friendly format will contribute to\nefforts, locally and nationally, to reduce waste, and will ensure the\ncustomer receives the information in the format most convenient to\nthem.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\nNone to the State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after\nit shall have become a law.\n\n\n",
